window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"11-04","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"11-04-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-01/","title_he":"א – אירוסין ונישואין","title_en":"1. Betrothal and Marriage","body_en":"<p>The concept of marriage, with all its spiritual and halakhic meanings, was introduced by the Torah. Before the giving of the Torah, the practice was that a man and woman who met and agreed to live together would enter the man's home and begin to live a married life without a wedding ceremony, and so children would be born. There was also no formal divorce ceremony; rather, when one of the spouses wanted to break the bond between them, he would separate and go his way. Once the Torah was given to Israel, we were commanded with a positive mitzva that if a man and woman want to live together, they should do so by kiddushin and ḥuppa, and likewise separation should be done by a bill of divorce (Rambam, Hilkhot Ishut 1:1).</p>\n<p>The wedding is made up of two stages: erusin and nisu'in, which are kiddushin and ḥuppa. The erusin is kiddushin, and the nisu'in is the ḥuppa. Admittedly, today people are accustomed to call the party at which the couple celebrate their decision to marry 'erusin' (engagement), and after the party the groom and bride are called engaged. But truly, according to halakha they are not betrothed but only matched, for the erusin is the kiddushin, that is, the stage at which the groom betroths the bride. In principle a man can betroth his wife in three ways: with money, with a document and with intercourse (Mishna Kiddushin 1:1). But in practice everyone is accustomed to betroth by acquisition with money, which is done by the groom giving the ring to the bride and saying the formula: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with this ring according to the law of Moshe and Israel.\" From the erusin onwards the bride is considered a married woman, and if they want to separate, the groom will need to give the bride a get. However, even after the erusin the groom and bride may not begin their life together, and only after the nisu'in, which takes place by their standing under the ḥuppa and the recitation of the seven blessings, are they permitted to begin to live together.</p>\n<p>In the past they were accustomed to wait twelve months between erusin and nisu'in, that is, between kiddushin and ḥuppa; and during that year the couple would prepare for the wedding emotionally and prepare the home, the clothes and the wedding meal. It is not hard to perceive the special beauty in the prolonged year-long waiting of the couple, who would look forward and long for the day of the wedding, on which they would begin to live together. That year would deepen the emotional and spiritual relationship between them.</p>\n<p>But over the course of time and the exiles, a need arose to unite the erusin with the nisu'in. First, there were cases in which, between the erusin and the nisu'in, the family of the groom or the bride was forced to flee its place, and so the couple remained tied to each other by a bond of kiddushin without the ability to realise it through nisu'in and without the ability to dissolve it through a get. And there were cases in which, between the erusin and the nisu'in, the couple were not careful to keep modesty between them. For these reasons the custom was accepted in Israel to unite the erusin and the nisu'in at one time, which is the wedding day. And so at the wedding both the erusin – the kiddushin with the ring – and the nisu'in – by setting up the ḥuppa – are held.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-02/","title_he":"ב – פסולי חיתון","title_en":"2. Those Unfit to Marry","body_en":"<p>Not every man and woman may marry each other. For example, a brother and sister are forbidden to marry each other. Likewise a man may not marry his aunt, but an uncle may marry his niece (as explained below 6:1-2; 6:5-7).</p>\n<p>It is a severe prohibition for a Jew to marry a gentile, whether the man is Jewish and the woman a gentile or the woman is Jewish and the man a gentile. If, Heaven forbid, they transgressed the prohibition, the law of the children is like the law of the mother: if she is Jewish, her children are Jewish and may marry Jews; and if she is a gentile, her children are gentiles and may not marry Jews.</p>\n<p>A Jewish son or daughter may not marry a mamzer or mamzeret, that is, one who was born of a severe prohibition, such as the prohibition of a married woman or incest within the family. Admittedly, a mamzer or mamzeret may marry each other or a male or female convert, but the children born to them will be considered mamzerim (below 6:9-10). And in any case a person should not decide in this law without thorough consultation with a rabbi.</p>\n<p>Male kohanim have additional marriage prohibitions, since they were consecrated to the service of the Temple. Therefore they may not marry: a convert, a divorcee, a ḥalutza, a ḥalala and a zona. A ḥalala is the daughter of a ḥalal, that is, the daughter of a kohen whose holiness was profaned because he was born to a kohen who married a woman forbidden to him, such as a divorcee. And so the law continues to the end of all generations: every daughter born to descendants of ḥalalim is forbidden to a kohen. A zona is a Jewish woman who had relations, willingly or by force, with a gentile or in incest within the family. Daughters of kohanim have no restrictions; rather, whoever is fit to marry a daughter of Israel is fit to marry a daughter of a kohen. Admittedly, daughters of kohanim were accustomed to try to marry kohanim or Israelites who are Torah scholars (Pesaḥim 49a).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-03/","title_he":"ג – קניין הקידושין","title_en":"3. The Acquisition of Kiddushin","body_en":"<p>One of the central differences between Judaism and the other religions is that Judaism strives for the complete repair of the world. The other religions despaired of repairing this world with all its physical components, and they call on their believers to detach themselves from the life of this world and to flee to a spiritual world that arose in their imagination, in order to be saved from the frustration and sorrow in the life of this world. But Judaism strives for a complete repair of the world. This idea is connected with faith in the One God, who created the world with all its spiritual and physical components; and from this follows the principle that every spiritual idea should have a concrete and practical expression in the world, and every practical thing should have a sanctified value that comes to expression in the mitzvot of the Torah. And so the world with all its components receives meaning and value. Therefore the covenant of marriage too is not expressed only in fine words and the expression of a noble will, but in kiddushin carried out by an acquisition with money that the groom gives the bride, so that the great idea of love will take effect on a concrete thing in this world. Without this, love is uprooted and detached from the practical and concrete sides of the world, and in the end it is lost and fades away.</p>\n<p>The acquisition of kiddushin is carried out by the groom giving the bride money, and in exchange she becomes consecrated to be his wife and forbidden to the rest of the world. Without the full consent of the bride the kiddushin do not take effect; and even if a man weighs out billions of shekels into a woman's hand and says to her: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with these billions of shekels according to the law of Moshe and Israel,\" as long as she did not agree to be betrothed, the kiddushin do not take effect. By contrast, if the groom gives the bride one peruta and says: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with this peruta according to the law of Moshe and Israel,\" and the bride agrees to be betrothed with a peruta, she is betrothed. And this is because the money of the kiddushin is not payment for the bride's worth, for no sum of money would equal her worth; rather there needs to be a concrete legal expression of the bride's passage from the status of an unmarried woman to the new status of a married woman. And already with one peruta a concrete legal expression is given to this.</p>\n<p>Since there is no need for the kiddushin to be done specifically with a coin, but one may also use something worth money, they were accustomed to betroth with a ring, which the bride can always wear on her hand, and which will serve her as a remembrance of her kiddushin (Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh 552). Many are accustomed to betroth with a ring of pure gold (Mordekhai), and some were accustomed to betroth with a silver ring (Ben Ish Ḥai, first year, Shofetim 8; Nisu'in Ke-Hilkhatam 7:6).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-04/","title_he":"ד – פרטי הלכות הקידושין","title_en":"4. Details of the Laws of Kiddushin","body_en":"<p>The acquisition with the ring must be for the sake of kiddushin; therefore before the groom gives the ring to the bride he must say to her: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with this ring according to the law of Moshe and Israel.\" And the bride holds out her finger, and thereby expresses her consent to the kiddushin; and the groom places the ring on his bride's finger, and with this the kiddushin are completed.</p>\n<p>So that the light-headed should not come to drunkenness on that day, as is customary among light-headed gentiles who revel in drunkenness and licentiousness before their wedding, in many communities grooms and brides were accustomed to fast on their wedding day. In addition, through the fast the grooms and brides are aroused to come to the wedding out of repentance and prayer. Also in communities that were not accustomed to fast, they are careful that the groom and bride not drink strong drink all the wedding day, so that their consent to the kiddushin will be with a clear mind.</p>\n<p>There must be no mistake at all in the bride's understanding of the acquisition of kiddushin; for if, for example, the bride thinks that the ring is worth a thousand shekels, and really it is worth only fifty shekels, it is possible that the kiddushin are invalid, because her intention was to be betrothed with an expensive ring and not with a simple ring. Therefore it is customary ideally to betroth with a ring without engravings and without precious stones, with which the possibility of a mistake about its value is limited. However, in practice, even if there are engravings and stones on the ring, as long as the bride agrees to be betrothed with it even if it is worth only a peruta, it is valid for kiddushin. To be free of all concern, there are rabbis who before the kiddushin ask the bride and the witnesses whether the ring is worth at least a peruta; and since they answer that it is worth at least a peruta, it is clear that there is no mistake in the kiddushin.</p>\n<p>The kiddushin are carried out by an act of acquisition, in that the groom transfers his ring to the bride. Therefore the ring must first belong to the groom; for this he must pay the seller for the ring before the wedding, because before he has paid for it the ring does not yet belong to him with a complete acquisition from the Torah, and the kiddushin are not complete (Avnei Milu'im 28:33). And if the groom's parents bought the ring with their money, they must transfer it to the groom's ownership by giving him the ring as a complete gift, and he should lift it at least three handbreadths above the ground and intend to acquire it from them by 'acquisition by lifting'.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-05/","title_he":"ה – העדים","title_en":"5. The Witnesses","body_en":"<p>Even if the groom gives his bride the ring and says to her: \"Behold, you are betrothed to me with this ring according to the law of Moshe and Israel,\" if valid witnesses are not present at the place, the kiddushin do not take effect and they are still considered single. And this is the fundamental difference between testimony in monetary law and testimony in kiddushin and divorce. The purpose of witnesses in monetary law is to clarify what truly happened: did so-and-so give a loan to so-and-so? And how much did he give? Or did so-and-so damage so-and-so's property, or steal? But when there is no dispute between the two sides, there is no need for witnesses.</p>\n<p>Not so is the law regarding kiddushin and divorce, in which the presence of the witnesses is an inseparable part of the act of kiddushin or divorce; and if there are no witnesses, even if both spouses tell that they performed kiddushin or divorce between them, it has no validity. For in kiddushin and divorce it is not merely a matter of a monetary obligation from one to the other, but of an essential change in their status that affects all people. From now on the woman is a married woman and forbidden to all other men, and whoever commits adultery with her is liable to death (below 6:4); and if she was divorced, she may marry any other man. Such a change in her status must be done in the presence of witnesses, who represent the whole congregation of Israel.</p>\n<p>Therefore, before the wedding one must choose two valid witnesses, whose role is to stand by the ḥuppa and see the whole order of the kiddushin. And although many people are present at the wedding, one must designate two valid witnesses; for many times around the ḥuppa stand relatives who are disqualified from being witnesses, while the friends who can be witnesses stand far away and do not observe attentively all the details of the kiddushin. Further, we are concerned for the view of several poskim who hold that if in one testimony valid and invalid witnesses were present with the thought that they would testify together, even the testimony of the valid ones is invalid. Therefore two witnesses are designated, and the rabbi who conducts the kiddushin says to them: \"Only you are the witnesses,\" that is: do not think that you are testifying here together with the relatives; only you are the witnesses. Thereby the relatives do not join the testimony, and consequently do not invalidate it. In addition, the witnesses to the kiddushin are also the witnesses who sign the ketuba; therefore one must designate them, and take care that they see the whole order of the kiddushin and the ḥuppa, and sign the ketuba.</p>\n<p>The witnesses must be valid, that is, adult males, who keep Torah and mitzvot, at least thirteen years old, who are not relatives of the groom or the bride. But brothers, uncles, cousins, grandfather and great-grandfather of the groom or the bride are disqualified from testimony, since they are relatives. Likewise a worker who works for the groom or the bride is disqualified from testifying at their wedding, because he depends on them for his salary. But good friends, between whom and one of the spouses there is no family relationship or financial dependence, can serve as witnesses.</p>\n<p>A person who does not identify with the values of Torah and mitzvot on which the kiddushin are based cannot be a witness at the wedding. Therefore a person who sins in sexual prohibitions is disqualified from testimony. Likewise a thief is disqualified from being a witness, and there is no difference between a person who breaks into his fellow's house at night and steals, and one who sells in a shop and alters the weight in his favour, or a factory manager who steals money from the factory's account into his pocket: all these are thieves and disqualified from testimony. Likewise a person who does not keep Shabbat according to halakha is not valid for testimony of kiddushin, which are based on the mitzvot of the Torah. Since through the witnesses the kiddushin and the marriage are carried out, it is customary ideally to be meticulous in choosing God-fearing witnesses. And some are particular to choose Torah scholars as witnesses.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-06/","title_he":"ו – הכתובה","title_en":"6. The Ketuba","body_en":"<p>Together with the joy of the wedding and the optimism accompanying the couple, halakha, in its responsible and sober view, did not forget to care for the rights of the woman in case the match does not turn out well. For this purpose the ketuba is written, in which the groom undertakes to give his wife a sum of money in case they divorce or he dies before her, so that she can subsist as a divorcee or a widow. Since a ketuba is already being written, the Sages further added and established in the text of the ketuba all the other obligations that the Torah imposed on the husband – to care for his wife's sustenance and all her needs. It turns out that the ketuba is the woman's charter of rights.</p>\n<p>The ketuba itself is worded in the form of a declaration of the witnesses, who testify to the groom's undertaking towards his bride. Since at the time the ketuba was formulated, in the days of the Second Temple, Aramaic was the spoken language, and consequently the common people understood Aramaic better than Hebrew, the Sages instituted the text of the ketuba in Aramaic, so that all grooms and brides would understand what it was about. Since then the text has been preserved even when Aramaic stopped serving as the spoken language. Let us translate the part that details the groom's obligations to the bride: \"We testify that the groom so-and-so son of so-and-so said to the bride so-and-so daughter of so-and-so: Be my wife according to the law of Moshe and Israel. And with the help of Hashem I will work for you, honour, feed, support, sustain and clothe you in the manner of Jewish men who support their wives faithfully… And I hereby undertake your sustenance, your clothing and the supply of your other needs, and to have marital relations with you in the way of all the earth.\" After this the sum of the ketuba is mentioned, which the groom undertakes to give the bride in case they divorce. And the witnesses conclude and declare: \"And the bride consented to the groom, and became his wife.\"</p>\n<p>One of the central roles of the ketuba is to prevent unnecessary divorces. For when the angry husband remembers the sum he will have to pay his wife if he divorces her, his mind will cool and he will think: perhaps it is better to try to live with her in peace and love, instead of paying her her ketuba. As the Sages said: \"So that it should not be easy in his eyes to send her away\" (Ketubot 39b). In order to secure the woman's rights, Shimon ben Shetaḥ instituted that all the husband's property be mortgaged for the payment of the ketuba. That is, even if the husband has no cash at the time of the divorce, his property will be seized and the value of the ketuba collected from it. This enactment too was intended to restrain angry husbands from divorcing in haste.<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>The sum of the ketuba is determined by three components: the main ketuba, the dowry, and the addition to the ketuba. The main ketuba is a fixed sum of two hundred zuz, with which one can subsist for at least a year. And if the bride was a divorcee or a widow, the main ketuba was a hundred zuz. The second part is the dowry: that is, one calculates the money and property that the bride's side transferred to the groom's possession, and since the groom gained it, he must add its value in the ketuba, so that if he divorces her, her property will not remain in his possession. The third part is the addition that the groom would add of his own to the main ketuba and the sum of the dowry he received. And the three components are calculated together, and this is the sum of the ketuba, that is, the sum the bride will receive in case the groom divorces her or dies before her.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"In tractate Ketubot (82b) the gradual process is explained, until the optimal point of balance was found. At first, so that it should not be easy in men's eyes to divorce their wives, they instituted that every husband, even if he is poor, in the case of divorce would pay his divorcee at least two hundred zuz, a sum that would hurt his pocket and make him think twice before jumping to divorce his wife.<br>But since it was not established that the husband's property is mortgaged for the payment of the ketuba, there were women who grew old and did not marry, because they feared that despite the undertaking in the ketuba, if the husband fell into economic difficulties he would divorce them and send them empty-handed from his home, because in practice he would have no money to pay them. Therefore they instituted that the ketuba money be deposited in the house of the woman's parents, and so even if the husband fell into difficulties, the sum of her ketuba was secured. But it became clear that when the money lay in cash in the house of the woman's parents, the men did not feel that it belonged to them, and in a time of anger more of them tended to divorce their wives. Therefore they instituted that the sum of the ketuba be kept in the couple's home and made into valuable vessels; and in the case of divorce the woman would take the vessels of her ketuba. But it became clear that in such a situation too the husbands did not feel a significant loss at the time of divorce, for these vessels were not considered theirs in their eyes, and in a time of crisis the man would relatively easily write a get and say to his wife: take your vessels and go out. Therefore Shimon ben Shetaḥ instituted that all the husband's property be mortgaged for the payment of the ketuba. And this was the optimal point of balance: for on the one hand the payment of the ketuba hurt the husband, since he needed to take cash out of his pocket or to sell some of his property to pay the ketuba; and on the other hand the payment of the ketuba was secured, for all the husband's property, including land and apartments he had at the time of the marriage and sold afterwards, was mortgaged for the payment of the ketuba (this enactment was also successful economically, for through it the ketuba money was invested in property that yielded fruits for the benefit of the family members)."}]},{"slug":"11-04-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-07/","title_he":"ז – משמעות סכום הכתובה בימינו","title_en":"7. The Meaning of the Ketuba Sum in Our Days","body_en":"<p>In the past there was enormous importance to the sum written in the ketuba. But today this sum has almost no meaning; and even if the groom and bride come, Heaven forbid, to divorce, the sum written in the ketuba will have little meaning, because the sages of the Rishonim accepted the two enactments of Rabbeinu Gershom Me'or Ha-Gola: a) a man may not marry two wives,<br>b) a man may not divorce his wife without her consent. These enactments turned the bowl upside down. Until then the power was in the hands of the husband: he could divorce his wife without her consent, and therefore it was necessary to care for the woman, that if he divorced her he would pay her the sum stated in the ketuba. In those days the negotiation over the sum of the ketuba was extremely significant, for through the size of the ketuba the bride's family secured her future, both by deterring the husband from divorcing her arbitrarily, and in case he nevertheless decided to divorce her, for then he would need to pay her a sum that would allow her reasonable subsistence after the divorce.</p>\n<p>But from the time Israel accepted the enactments of Rabbeinu Gershom, the husband cannot divorce his wife without her consent. And as long as she does not agree to divorce, he cannot bind himself to another woman, since he may not be married to two wives. Therefore even if a paltry sum is written in her ketuba, as long as she does not consent to divorce, the husband cannot cut off his ties with her, and so the woman can demand of her husband the sum she considers reasonable in exchange for her consent to receive the get.<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>That is, from the acceptance of the enactments of Rabbeinu Gershom, divorce depends on negotiation between the spouses; and if the man's wish to divorce his wife is stronger than her wish to divorce, he will usually need to pay her the price of the ketuba or more. And if the reverse, the woman wants to divorce more than the man, presumably she will forgo part of the ketuba for him, and perhaps even pay him for agreeing to divorce her. In very rare cases, where the woman is in no way willing to accept a get, and in the view of the court the family situation between them requires divorce, if a hundred rabbis agree to it, the husband will be permitted to marry another wife.</p>\n<p>Also in the case of the husband's death the sum written in the ketuba has no meaning, because for many years the custom has been that the man and the woman are partners in their property, and so too is the law; consequently when the husband dies the woman receives at least half of their joint property, which is usually more than the value of the ketuba. Therefore today there is no reason for the families of the groom and bride to argue with each other about the size of the sum of the ketuba. Only in a case where the groom offers an especially low sum, or the bride's side demands an especially high sum, is there room to be concerned that these sums will serve as a basis for negotiation if they divorce; and therefore the other side should stand firm and demand that the sum of the ketuba be reasonable, as is customary among most couples of their standing.</p>\n<p>Further on, the differences between the status of the man and the woman and the changes that have taken place in this, and the importance of the ketuba in our days, will be explained in greater detail. Meanwhile we will continue to explain the laws of the ketuba.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Not only this, but since then the bride's side usually needed to pay a larger sum for the wedding, because the groom's family was not willing for the groom to undertake to support his wife and in addition not be able to divorce her without her consent; and so, at its demand, the sum in the ketuba was low, and against it the bride's family needed to pay large sums for the wedding. And the more successful the groom and the more established his family, or the harder the period economically, the more the bride's side needed to pay; to the point that a situation arose in which poor parents could not marry off their daughters, and they had to go from door to door for 'hakhnasat kalla'."}]},{"slug":"11-04-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-08/","title_he":"ח – דיני הכתובה","title_en":"8. The Laws of the Ketuba","body_en":"<p>The Tanna'im disagreed on the question whether the Torah obligated the husband to write his wife a ketuba defining the sum of compensation he would need to give his wife in case they divorce, or whether this is an enactment of the Sages, continuing the mitzva of the Torah that imposed on the groom to care for all the bride's needs (Ketubot 10a). In any case, because of the great importance of the ketuba, which secures the rights of the woman, the Sages forbade a man to stay with his wife even one hour without a ketuba (SA EH 66:3). And if the ketuba was lost, the husband is obligated to write his wife a new ketuba immediately. Many brides were accustomed to deposit their ketuba in their parents' home. In the religious councils in the State of Israel they instituted writing the ketuba in two copies: the first copy remains in the bride's possession, and the second is deposited in the religious council, so that if the ketuba that remained with the bride or her parents is lost, the groom and bride will not need to separate from each other until a new ketuba document is written, but can continue to live together on the basis of the ketuba deposited in the religious council. And some are not satisfied with this solution, because in their view the ketuba may not be written in two copies; and then they write explicitly on the additional document that it is a copy, and thereby it has no validity, and if the original ketuba document is lost, they may not continue to live together until they write a new ketuba.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>The ketuba document must be drawn up according to all the halakhic rules and precisions, so that there will be no doubt about its validity. The names of the groom and bride must be written precisely, and if they have several names, all of them must be mentioned. Likewise their fathers' names must be mentioned, and in the fathers' names too one must be precise. If they erred in writing their name or their fathers' names, the ketuba is invalid. Likewise one must be precise in writing the date of the ketuba obligation, for if they wrote an earlier date, the ketuba is invalid. Therefore they are careful not to hold the ḥuppa at bein ha-shmashot, because it is doubtful whether it belongs to the previous day or to the new day, and it is impossible to write a precise date in the ketuba. Likewise one must state the city or settlement in which the wedding is held, for without stating the place the document is invalid.</p>\n<p>After the document has been written properly, one must perform a kinyan, that is, an act through which the groom becomes obligated in practice in everything written in the ketuba. The kinyan is performed by 'kinyan ḥalifin', that is, the rabbi gives the groom as a gift a handkerchief or another object, and in exchange the groom becomes obligated in everything written in the ketuba, and the rabbi acquires for the bride all her rights. Afterwards, under the ḥuppa, the groom gives the ketuba to the bride in the presence of the witnesses, and with this the process of his undertaking the ketuba is completed.</p>\n<p>The ketuba is worded as a declaration of the witnesses, who testify how they saw the groom betroth the bride and undertake towards her everything written in the ketuba. Therefore they must sign at the end of the document. It is customary that the groom too adds his signature to the ketuba, but by law the signature of the witnesses is sufficient.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In a case where they forgot to bring the ketuba to the wedding, and it is not possible to bring a ketuba to the wedding within a reasonable time, in a time of pressing need, so as not to delay all the guests, one may be lenient and hold the ḥuppa even before the ketuba is written, on condition that the groom undertakes orally all the conditions of the ketuba and performs a kinyan on this before the witnesses. And as soon as they bring the ketuba, the rabbi should fill in the details that need to be written in it, and they should again perform a kinyan on everything written in the ketuba, and the witnesses should sign the ketuba, and the groom should give the ketuba to the bride."}]},{"slug":"11-04-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-09/","title_he":"ט – מעמד האיש והאישה בנישואין","title_en":"9. The Standing of the Man and the Woman in Marriage","body_en":"<p>Some ask why, in the process of marriage, the man betroths and the woman is betrothed; the groom spreads his protection over the bride and brings her into his home, and takes responsibility to care for all her needs and her happiness, as in the custom of the veil, the ḥuppa and the yiḥud room (below 5:4-5; 12). In addition, we have learned (in halakhot 6-7) that over time changes took place in the standing of the man and the woman and in the laws of marriage, the ketuba and divorce, and the question is why. To try to understand all this one must go back to the root.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>At first two faces were included in Adam Ha-Rishon, of male and of female, joined back to back, with the male face more prominent than the female face; therefore when they were separated, the consciousness of Adam Ha-Rishon remained with the male. And therefore he was the one who sought Ḥava, who had been separated from him, and said (Bereshit 2:23): \"This time it is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she shall be called woman, because she was taken out of man.\" Something like the story of Adam Ha-Rishon happens with every couple. In their supernal root their souls are joined, and in the process of their descent into the world the soul of the female was separated from the male; therefore he is the one who must seek his lost object (Kiddushin 2b), and therefore he betroths his wife and brings her into the ḥuppa, and on him is the mitzva to initiate the union with her in order to gladden her within the mitzva of ona and for the sake of the mitzva of 'be fruitful and multiply'; and the woman's mitzva is to be betrothed, to open herself and to receive the groom.</p>\n<p>After every stage that the man initiates, the woman, in receiving her husband, makes the bond between them deep and complete, and raises it to a higher level. If we look closely, we will find that the man's power is in the first initiative, in breaking the way, in courting and in kiddushin. But after the man has betrothed his wife, sometimes his power weakens, and the woman's tendency is then to invest more in the bond between them and to deepen it. This is expressed also in that the woman receives the man's seed and continues to develop it throughout the pregnancy until the birth. From the revealed side, the male is first, leading and guiding, and therefore he is the one who must initiate the relationship. On the other hand, since the male is considered the continuation of Adam Ha-Rishon and the woman of Ḥava, in a certain respect the female expresses a higher stage. For the material from which man was created is dust from the ground, whereas the woman was formed from man (Bereshit 2:7; 2:21-23). And the Sages said (Eruvin 18a) that Hashem built the woman in a special way in order to make her beautiful, as it is said: \"And the Lord God built the side that He had taken from the man into a woman.\" Therefore the woman has the power to deepen the bond between them and raise it to a higher level.</p>\n<p>The man's power is more revealed and outward, and the woman's power is hidden and inward. The order of the world is that first the outward side is revealed and afterwards the inward side. And so at first the man's standing is higher: he courts, betroths and arouses to the mitzvot of ona and 'be fruitful and multiply'. But as the years go by, thanks to the woman's virtue, who can receive his initiative, develop it and make it something complete, her standing rises; to the point that, on a deep look, many times one finds that her influence is greater than the man's.</p>\n<p>If we go deeper, we will find that just as the man's initiative in the first stage moves the woman in secret in all her activity in the second stage, so too the woman moves the first stage in secret. Admittedly, the revealed, active courting is done by the man, but the woman, by her very good and beautiful existence, draws him to court her. Admittedly, the man is the one who betroths the woman, but the woman, in her deep will to create a partnership and a family, brings him to this. And there are cases in which the man wants to join with a woman without a long commitment, and only because the woman does not agree to join with him without a binding marriage does he marry her.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The ideas in these sections are brought from PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 10:2-10, and there I noted in note 1 that the essential differences between male and female described here are those that appear to us from nature and from halakha; but it is possible that the process of historical change that will be explained further on will also lead to the understanding of further aspects that will leave these understandings of ours in the shade. Also today there are people with an egalitarian-feminist outlook who believe in the Torah and in the mitzva of marriage, and nevertheless do not agree with these distinguishing explanations, either because in their view they generalise too much or because they disagree entirely with the position accepted in the tradition according to which there are essential differences between man and woman. And their proof is from what the Sages said (Kiddushin 35a): \"Scripture made woman equal to man for all the laws in the Torah.\" I too hesitated whether to include these ideas in the book, both because their essence is in the philosophical side of the Torah, whereas the main purpose of this book is to clarify the halakha and its reasons, and because they were not defined clearly in the words of the Sages. But I decided in favour, because in my opinion they are correct, and through them many mitzvot are explained, and through them the historical process is explained, and their foundation is in the words of the Sages and the Kabbalah, and they are also very dear to my wife and to me. In addition, even if we accept some of the positions of the egalitarian-feminist outlook, the conception of the classical structure of partnership is very deeply rooted in the depths of souls; and the attempt to create today an egalitarian partnership, in which the man is exempt from the role of the one who leads and courts, and the woman is exempt from the role of the one who receives and is moved, causes in practice many young people not to succeed in reaching marriage. And of those who reached marriage, a high percentage break their covenant in betrayals and divorces. Therefore, even for those who hold that one must rise to an egalitarian structure of partnership without essential differences between the roles of the man and the woman, it is necessary to study well the classical structure of partnership and its meanings, and on it to build the next storey; for if they lose the good in it, they will not succeed in making a covenant of partnership of love and holiness. See further there in PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato chapter 10, where the ideas brought here are expanded a little more."}]},{"slug":"11-04-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-10/","title_he":"י – עליית מעמד האשה במסגרת הנישואין","title_en":"10. The Rise of the Woman's Standing Within Marriage","body_en":"<p>Of the decisive influence of the woman the Sages said (Bereshit Rabba 17:7): \"It once happened that a pious man was married to a pious woman, and they did not raise children from each other. They said: we are of no benefit to the Holy One, blessed be He. They rose and divorced each other. He went and married a wicked woman, and she made him wicked; she went and married a wicked man, and she made him righteous. So everything comes from the woman.\" It is likely that in both these cases the woman's influence was hidden. Had they argued with each other about the proper way, the man would have insisted on going his opposite way and rejected his wife's words. But their influence was inward. The righteous woman, out of humility and love, responded to every good initiative of her wicked husband, rejoiced in the good point in it, broadened it and went deeply into it, and so the man was drawn after her, for all the good things she developed came from himself. Until, in a gradual process, his bad sides were pushed aside and disappeared, and his good sides grew strong and became the main thing. By contrast, the wicked woman did not respond to the righteous man's good initiatives, and when there was a negative point in his initiative, she immediately responded to it gladly, and developed and broadened it, until in a gradual process she made him wicked. Admittedly, usually there are no great differences between the spouses, and both are directed to a common goal; and through the mutual enrichment between them they go on rising, with the man initiating and the woman continuing his initiative, deepening and developing it. And out of this he again initiates and she again develops, and so on in turn. And sometimes they change places, and the development turns out to be initiative and the initiative development; but the main melody remains in most couples, with the value of the woman's role being revealed more over the years and her standing rising.</p>\n<p>An allusion to this we find in the words of the Sages about the relationship between the Holy One, blessed be He, and Israel. \"A parable of a king who had an only daughter, and he loved her exceedingly and would call her 'my daughter'. He did not cease loving her until he called her 'my sister' (as an equal to him); he did not cease loving her until he called her 'my mother' (who is above him). So the Holy One, blessed be He, at first called Israel 'daughter'… He did not cease loving them until He called them 'My sister'… He did not cease loving them until He called them 'My mother'\" (Shemot Rabba 52:5).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-11/","title_he":"יא – התהליך ההיסטורי","title_en":"11. The Historical Process","body_en":"<p>Just as within every couple a process takes place in which the woman's standing rises, so too is the historical process: at first the man's standing was distinctly higher, and in a gradual process the woman's standing went on rising. This process is intended for the repair of the world, for Hashem created the world lacking so that human beings would merit to be partners in its repair. In the first stage there is a need to define good and evil and to set the basic direction. At this stage the male trait is more useful, for he tends more to separate between the various areas, and between reason and emotion, while focusing on the challenge before him; whereas the trait of the woman, who tends to combine the areas, may interfere with defining the goals, since emotion may interfere in rational considerations and bend them for the bad, as happened in the sin of Adam Ha-Rishon. In a further way one can explain that the man tends more to reason, which by its nature is more objective and analytical, whereas the woman tends more to a consideration that includes emotion, which by its nature is more subjective and able to receive and contain. Therefore in the first stage, in which one must decide and determine what is good and what is evil, the man must lead and guide, and he does this with the help of Torah study, and thereby the world goes on progressing. And the more the values of good and evil become clearer and more accepted, the more we progress to a stage in which one must understand the values more deeply, with all their shades, and realise them in the fullness of real life; and then the trait of the woman becomes more important (see PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 10, n. 2).</p>\n<p>And so it is alluded to in the hidden wisdom: in this world the man's standing is higher; in the time to come there will be equality; and in the World to Come the woman's standing will be higher. This world is full of wickedness, quarrels and wars, and in order to fight its bad sides the man must study Torah, clarify the foundations of truth and goodness, and make the values of truth and goodness rule in all the arrangements of life. Also in the period called 'this world' a process of rise in the woman's standing takes place, and the more the values of the good are clarified, the more her standing rises; but still, outwardly, the man's standing is higher, because there is still a need for war to make the values of the good rule. (Admittedly, already in this world, in the hidden level of the inner world of the spirit, the level of equality is revealed. And even more, in the depth of the world of the soul, the level is revealed in which the woman is higher and more influential.)</p>\n<p>The second stage, of the time to come, is the time of the redemption, in which the people of Israel return to their Land and the Temple is built, and the light of Torah and faith illuminates the whole world, and Mashiaḥ son of David reigns over Israel, \"and he shall judge between the nations, and shall decide for many peoples; and they shall beat their swords into ploughshares, and their spears into pruning hooks; nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more\" (Yeshayahu 2:4). And then the standing of the man and the woman will be equal. There will still be a need to define the difference between good and evil and to make the good rule, and for this there is more need for the man's traits. But on the other hand, we will already reach the stage in which we reveal in practice the values of faith and goodness in real life, with all its components, and for this there is more need for the woman's traits, and thereby her standing will be equal to the man's standing.</p>\n<p>The third stage, of the World to Come, is the world after the resurrection of the dead, in which there will no longer be a need for a struggle between good and evil; and then the feminine traits will be able to be revealed in their fullness, and the woman's standing will rise above the man's standing, as alluded to in the verse (Yirmiyahu 31:21): \"For the Lord has created a new thing in the earth: a woman shall go round a man.\" Admittedly, it seems that the first role will always remain with the men: of the one who defines the principle, who courts his wife, and betroths her as a wife, and begins to gladden her in the mitzva of ona. But then the view will be deeper, and it will become clear that the feminine virtue is more important, for it is able to receive and reveal all that is hidden in the man's initiative, more than he himself understands. This ability also makes it possible to receive the Divine sparks hidden in creation, in a deeper, richer and more complete way. And this is the main purpose of the creation of the world, and all the precise definitions of the men in their Talmud are nothing but the breaking of the way towards the complete revelation in which the women will be greater partners. Therefore their standing then will be higher. This should not lessen the woman's modesty and humility, for precisely thanks to these traits her extra virtue is revealed, that she is able to receive everything with extra depth.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-12/","title_he":"יב – ירידת מעמד האשה בעקבות חטא אדם הראשון","title_en":"12. The Decline in the Woman's Standing Following the Sin of Adam Ha-Rishon","body_en":"<p>Beyond the fact that according to the order of the creation of man, in the first stage the man's standing is higher, following the sin of Adam Ha-Rishon, in which Ḥava was tempted to eat from the Tree of Knowledge and gave also to her husband to eat with her, a further fall took place in the woman's standing (Bereshit 3:16-19). Three main punishments were decreed upon them, and all three harmed the woman more. The first: the process of pregnancy, birth and raising babies became bound up with suffering and pains, weakened the woman's strength and required her to stay close to the home, depending on her husband to provide her with food, shelter and protection. The second: after their expulsion from the Garden of Eden, obtaining food became hard and dependent on physical strength, in which the man's advantage is prominent. Also the need to build a house that would protect them from rain and cold, and the need to defend themselves against enemies and beasts of prey, increased the woman's dependence on her husband. The third: death was decreed upon them, and precisely at the time of birth, at the stage in which the woman merits to bring new life into the world, her danger increased. And all that then remained to the woman in childbirth was to hope that if she died her husband would continue to care for her children.</p>\n<p>These punishments, with all the suffering in them, bring man to his repair. Following the sin it became clear that the feminine trait, which combines and mixes all the areas in its considerations, may lead to sin. For in this way the emotions, and among them the evil inclinations, interfere in thought and distort judgement. And the way to repair must be done by separating reason from emotion, while lowering the standing of the emotions, so that reason can weigh each matter on its own and rule over emotion and inclination. There is a lack in this, for in the complete state all the powers should come to expression in full harmony; but in order to establish the principles of justice and morality, one must first make reason rule over emotion and inclination. And since the man by his nature tends more to reason and to the ability to separate between the areas, which is the trait required to win the war of the inclination, the man was commanded to study Torah and to engage in the roles of leadership. And the more the world is repaired and the Torah illuminates its way, the less concern there will be that natural emotion will break out in a mistaken and destructive way; consequently the restraints on its expression will be removed, and the good in the feminine trait, which combines all the areas together, will come to expression, and the woman's standing will go on rising.</p>\n<p>The process of moral repair takes place in parallel with the development of science, technology and the economy, which has enabled man to earn his bread with less toil, and redeemed him from the pains of his punishment, and the woman from her desperate dependence on her husband. This process continued gradually for thousands of years, and in recent generations it has grown very strong through the creation of the possibility of earning a living with the help of talent without the need for physical strength. Also the development of medical science has greatly reduced the dangers of childbirth, and contributed to the rise in the woman's standing.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-13/","title_he":"יג – ההנהגה הגלויה והנסתרת בעולם הזה","title_en":"13. The Revealed and the Hidden Leadership in This World","body_en":"<p>Admittedly, even in periods in which the man's standing was at its height, on a deep look one could discern the great influence of the women. For even when the revealed leadership is given to the men, the great virtue of the women, which is connected to the time to come and the World to Come, is hidden in the depths and gives strength and power to the men to fulfil their role. Admittedly, for defining the goals and breaking the way the leadership was given into the hands of the men; but in great hours, in which there was a need for a deeper and more complete connection to faith, it was precisely the women who succeeded in meeting the task more than the men. Thus we find that the women clung to their faith and did not sin in the sin of the Golden Calf, \"and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave them their reward in this world, that they keep Rosh Ḥodesh more than the men. And He gave them a reward in the World to Come, that they are destined to be renewed like the new moons, as it is said (Tehillim 103:5): Who satisfies your old age with good, so that your youth is renewed like the eagle\" (Pirkei De-Rabbi Eliezer 45).</p>\n<p>Also when the men sinned in the sin of the spies and were afraid to enter the Land and fight for it, the women did not support them and did not take part in their sin, and therefore it was decreed only on the men that they would not enter the Land, and not on the women. And likewise we find that the daughters of Tzelofḥad loved the Land and wanted to inherit it (Tanḥuma Pinḥas 7). And likewise \"Rabbi Akiva expounded: in the merit of righteous women Israel went out of Egypt\" (Sota 11b; PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 1:8). And of the future they said: \"The generations are redeemed only in the merit of the righteous women of the generation\" (Midrash Zuta, Rut 4:11). And likewise we find in the stories of many families of the modern era that women were the ones who decided on the decision to make aliya to the Land and not to leave it, and thereby saved their families from the Holocaust and from assimilation. One can say that because the men are responsible for the leadership of the Torah in the present, women who accept their leadership are able out of this to connect to the great vision and to withstand great tests.</p>\n<p>Therefore at the giving of the Torah the address was to the women first, as it is said (Shemot 19:3): \"Thus shall you say to the house of Yaakov, and tell the children of Israel.\" And the Sages explained: \"say to the house of Yaakov\" – these are the women, to whom you shall speak in a gentle language; \"and tell the children of Israel\" – these are the men, to whom you shall speak in a harsh language suited to explaining the punishments of the Torah and its precise details. And from what is said, \"Thus shall you say,\" they learned that one must be careful about the order, that is, that you speak first to the women (Mekhilta, and brought in Rashi). For the great vision of the Torah is that with a gentle saying everything will be understood and repaired; and although we will reach this only in the future, and meanwhile the men's leadership must come first, at the giving of the Torah the women must come first, for they express the complete vision.</p>\n<p>And likewise we find that women have an advantage in their general relationship to the Torah, that they love and honour it more, because they belong more to the general, supernal side of the Torah (Siḥot Ha-Ratziyah, Shemot, pp. 178-181). Similarly the Sages said (Berakhot 17a): \"Greater is the promise that the Holy One, blessed be He, promised to women than to men\" (see PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 10:7). It follows that outwardly the standing of the men who study Torah is higher, but inwardly the connection of the women to the Torah is deeper, and it comes to expression in their encouraging their husbands and sons to study Torah; and in their merit Torah study endures in Israel.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-14/","title_he":"יד – תהליך העלייה במעמד האשה מבחינה הלכתית","title_en":"14. The Process of the Rise in the Woman's Standing in Halakha","body_en":"<p>The process of the rise in the woman's standing is expressed in halakha. By the essential law of the Torah, a man may marry several wives, and by his exclusive decision he can divorce his wife. Admittedly, he cannot betroth a woman without her agreeing to it, but he can divorce her with a get without her consent.</p>\n<p>To explain the halakha one must first say that the Torah does not compel a person to go against nature; rather nature is the infrastructure and the base on which a person must repair and complete himself. Therefore the Torah does not interfere in the economic market, but lets market forces do their work, while it sets moral limits and a vision of values. Therefore the Torah also did not forbid slavery, because in periods of want and hunger it is better for a person to be a slave, provided he can subsist. Without the framework of slavery, those people who did not succeed in supporting themselves – because they were lazy or lacked resourcefulness, or because their land was conquered – would have perished. Through slavery their chance of surviving and raising descendants, who today are free, increased. Therefore the Torah was content with setting moral limits to slavery, which marked the direction of moral progress towards a state of abolishing slavery at a time when there would be no existential necessity for it.</p>\n<p>So too regarding marriage: in a situation in which not everyone succeeded in supporting themselves properly, if men who succeeded by their resourcefulness in obtaining more food and property had not been allowed to marry two wives and even more, many women who would not have merited a husband able to support them would have died of hunger without raising descendants. Beyond this, when livelihood involved very hard work, and the man was required to toil all the hours of the day for his wife and children, to provide them with food, clothing and shelter, it was not possible to bind him to his wife. The fact that the man was permitted to divorce his wife without her consent or to marry an additional wife gave him the feeling of freedom that enabled him to undertake to bear the heavy burden of supporting her and supporting their small children.</p>\n<p>Admittedly, the Torah commanded that even if he marries an additional wife, he must take care to continue to gladden each of his wives in the mitzva of ona and to care for all their needs, as it is said (Shemot 21:10): \"Her food, her clothing and her conjugal rights he shall not diminish.\" In addition, it was established that a man should not marry a woman without a ketuba, that is, that he should undertake at the time of his marriage that if he divorces his wife he will compensate her with a sum of money sufficient for her subsistence for at least a year, so that it should not be easy in his eyes to divorce her (Ketubot 39b; Rambam, Ishut 10:7; above halakhot 6-8).</p>\n<p>Usually the sum of the ketuba was higher, as agreed in the negotiation between the groom and the bride's family. In a case where the sum of the ketuba was very high, and it became clear to the man that he had married a bad wife who caused him distress, his life was hard and bad, because he was obligated to care for all her needs without having the ability to divorce her (Yevamot 63b).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-15/","title_he":"טו – איסור שתי נשים","title_en":"15. The Prohibition of Two Wives","body_en":"<p>Over the generations the economic situation went on improving, and in parallel the cases of marrying two wives became very few, until already in the period of the Mishna, about two thousand years ago, no rabbis or sages who had two wives are mentioned. In the period of the Talmud, about one thousand six hundred years ago, the practice that a man does not marry a second wife in addition to his wife was so clear that the Amora'im disagreed whether in practice a man may marry an additional wife in addition to his wife. In the view of Rabbi Ami it is forbidden, because every woman who was betrothed to her husband did so with the understanding that he would not marry an additional wife. However, the halakha was established according to the view of Rava, that since the husband did not undertake this explicitly, he may betroth an additional wife without the consent of the first (Yevamot 65a).</p>\n<p>About a thousand years ago, Rabbeinu Gershom Me'or Ha-Gola in Ashkenaz instituted that a man should not marry more than one wife. And he further instituted that a man should not divorce his wife without her consent; and only in a time of pressing need, by a special permission of a hundred rabbis from three lands, would he be permitted to divorce her against her will.</p>\n<p>This enactment was not accepted in Spain as a binding enactment, but in practice this was the custom in most communities, the groom undertaking in the ketuba not to marry an additional wife in addition to his wife. After the founding of the State, in 1950 (5710), the Council of the Chief Rabbinate decided that the prohibition of two wives would apply to all communities.</p>\n<p>Seemingly one should ask: we hold the rule that the Sages have no authority to forbid something explicitly permitted in the Torah (Taz YD 117:1); if so, how could Rabbeinu Gershom forbid marrying two wives, and why was his enactment accepted in Israel? My teacher and master Rabbi Tzvi Yehuda Ha-Kohen Kook ztz\"l explained that from the context in which it is said in the Torah that it is permitted to marry two wives, one can understand that the matter is not desirable, as it is said (Devarim 21:15): \"If a man has two wives, one loved and the other hated, and they have borne him sons, the loved and the hated, and the firstborn son is the hated one's.\" The first problem is that one is loved and one is hated; and likewise we find that two wives are called rivals to each other. And out of this they may come to inheritance disputes that will tear the family apart, to the point that the Torah needed to warn: \"He may not make the son of the loved one the firstborn before the son of the hated one, who is the firstborn\" (ibid. 16). After that the case of the 'wayward and rebellious son' is brought, and the Sages explained that this comes to teach us that the son's corrupt state was caused by the man having two wives (Rashi, Devarim 21:11; Siḥot Ha-Ratziyah, Devarim, p. 361). Not only this, but in all the places where the Torah tells of two wives, it tells that problems followed from it. It follows that the permission to marry two wives is after the fact, because of a necessity that cannot be condemned. But when the necessity departed, Israel became accustomed not to marry two wives.</p>\n<p>It is fitting to note that the instructions of halakha went together with the improvement in men's ability to earn a living, and preceded by hundreds of years the rise in the woman's standing economically. For only in the modern period, with the perfection of machines and the move from hard physical work to work that requires more intellectual and emotional talent, did the level of women's income rise and their economic standing improve. That is, it was not the economic power of women and their struggle for their standing that caused the change in the halakhic instruction, but the improvement in the economic situation of the population as a whole. Therefore, from the time it was possible to sustain the women without the permission to marry two wives, the matter was forbidden absolutely, as it was in Ashkenaz, or became a rare phenomenon in most communities of Spanish origin. And so it was in the time of the Mishna regarding the enactment of the ketuba: from the time it was possible to require the man to give a ketuba with all the obligations in it, so it was established.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-16/","title_he":"טז – חיובי הנישואין והכתובה","title_en":"16. The Obligations of Marriage and the Ketuba","body_en":"<p>After all these introductions, let us return to the laws of marriage. At the time of marriage the man and the woman undertake to unite in love and joy, with each of the spouses responsible for the happiness and well-being of the other. Until the modern period, livelihood involved hard physical work in which the man had a great advantage. And in addition, the housework and caring for the children, which were imposed on the woman, took many hours, for everything was done by hand, including bringing water, preparing bread and food, and sewing and knitting the clothes. Therefore, in order to care for the woman's happiness and well-being, the man needed to undertake at the time of marriage to provide his wife with all her needs, as it is said (Shemot 21:10): \"Her food, her clothing and her conjugal rights he shall not diminish.\" This includes caring for her food, clothes, housing, furniture, medicines and medical treatment when she is ill, all according to the level accepted in their place and their family (PH Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 1:2).</p>\n<p>In exchange for the man's undertaking to care for all his wife's needs, the woman undertook to care for all the needs of the home and caring for the children; likewise she undertook that any money she earned, and likewise the property she brought with her from her parents' home, would be in her husband's possession. Since this agreement was intended for the woman's benefit, if the woman wanted she could cancel it according to the rule: \"I will not be maintained and I will not work\" (Ketubot 58b), that is: you are not obligated to care for my needs, and on the other hand everything I earn will be mine.<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>In addition, the groom must give the bride a ketuba, and in it an undertaking to pay the bride from his property the sum written in the ketuba in case he divorces her or dies before her. The sum of the undertaking was an amount on which one can live for a year, and usually they wrote higher sums in the ketuba, taking into account the man's wealth and the woman's standing (as explained above in halakha 6). The sum set in the ketuba was important and sometimes fateful at the time when they practised according to the essential law of the Torah, by which a man may marry several wives and divorce his wife against her will, for it served as a shield and barrier against divorces that would leave the woman destitute. But after the enactment of Rabbeinu Gershom, by which a man may not marry two wives, and likewise a man may not divorce his wife without her consent, the sum written in the ketuba is only a basis for negotiation (as explained above in halakha 7).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"These are the basic rules, in order to prevent quarrels. Admittedly, usually there were no disputes, and the husband would manage the family's business while sharing his decisions with his wife; and the more the wife understood in the economic field, the greater her influence grew, and in cases where it became clear that she was more talented in organisation and business, the management of the finances was in her hands. (In Ashkenaz they even wrote explicitly in the betrothal document that the spouses undertake to manage their business in partnership without hiding anything from each other.)"}]},{"slug":"11-04-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-17/","title_he":"יז – חיובי הנישואין היום","title_en":"17. The Obligations of Marriage Today","body_en":"<p>As we learned in the previous halakha, the basic undertaking in marriage was that the man is responsible for livelihood and all the money is in his hands, whereas the woman is responsible for the housework and caring for the children, and the man is obligated to provide his wife with all her needs, since she has no ability to do so. Since this whole arrangement was intended to protect the woman, if she preferred that her husband not support her and not provide her needs – for example, if she had property from her parents' home, or knew how to earn in trade – she could say to her husband: \"I will not be maintained and I will not work,\" and from then on all her earnings were her own, and he was exempt from providing all her needs. And the obligations of marriage remained in the emotional sphere, to gladden each other in the mitzva of ona and in married life. Admittedly, this was a very rare case.</p>\n<p>In recent generations, thanks to technological improvements, such as the possibility of getting water from the tap and buying food and clothing cheaply, the time required for housework decreased, and in the free time women began to work and earn a living. With the money they earned they could buy additional help with the housework, and likewise pay for a nursery for babies, and so additional time was created that they could devote to work. In parallel, the education system for boys and girls improved and gave women tools to integrate into many more profitable branches of the economy. So in a gradual process women's salaries rose, until today some of them earn more than their husbands.</p>\n<p>Despite all the far-reaching changes in the economic and social standing of the woman, also in our days the man's undertaking in the ketuba is very important, for two reasons. The first: the basic structure of most families is that the man is more responsible for livelihood and the woman more for caring for the children and the home. The second: the man's undertaking is also for the children, and if the man does not undertake towards his wife, there is a concern that he will flee from his parental responsibility. It is a fact that modern life has harmed the stability of the family unit, to the point that a considerable percentage of the children growing up today in Western countries grow up with their mother alone, without their father being present in their lives. Therefore the man's undertaking towards his wife at the time of marriage is so important.</p>\n<p>In any case, the changes in the woman's standing raise new questions regarding the division of responsibility for supporting the family, caring for the children and the housework, in days of marriage and, Heaven forbid, also in days of divorce. The guiding principle should be keeping the rules of justice between the spouses, while allowing divorce in hard cases, and on the other hand delaying hasty divorces and encouraging the keeping of the marriage. We will need to go through a period of feeling our way and trials until the halakhic position stabilises, similar to the process of the enactment of the ketuba in the days of the Second Temple (above halakha 6, and note 1). One change that has already been made is the sharing of the woman in the responsibility for the children's maintenance.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In families in which the women earn more than their husbands, the risk of divorce increases. But experience proves that when the women keep the traditional structure, by which the husband is responsible for leading his family although she earns more than he does, and he gives her the honour of being responsible for the running of the home, the chance that they will succeed in keeping their partnership in love and joy is higher. The intention is not that in practice it is necessary that he lead the home's finances, for if his wife understands more, it is better that she lead; rather the intention is that she give him the honour to decide and determine should he wish. Likewise regarding the running of the home: it is possible that in practice the man will manage it more, but he should give his wife the honour to decide and determine should she wish. One can say that the first melody should be according to the first order, in which the man leads and courts; and even if in practice the woman initiated, in some place she should give the man the place of the leader. Out of this first melody one can go on to develop in many and varied directions – to a state in which the woman leads, or to an egalitarian state, or to a state in which in certain areas the man leads and in certain areas the woman – but from time to time it is fitting to return to the first, basic melody.<br>It seems that even those who reject the traditional essential differences will admit that within the framework of the cultural construction in which we live, the traditional family structure has a better chance of succeeding, whereas a rapid change towards equality greatly increases the risk of undermining the partnership and of divorce; and it is not fitting to endanger the peace of the partnership on the altar of equality. Admittedly, in their view it is possible that in a gradual process more refined and deeper family patterns will be found for keeping the partnership with a different division of roles. And time will tell whether they are right, and in what."}]},{"slug":"11-04-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-18/","title_he":"יח – גירושין","title_en":"18. Divorce","body_en":"<p>Despite the holiness in marriage, the Torah established that it is possible to divorce, and in hard cases it is even a mitzva. For the aim of marriage is that the spouses live in love and joy, peace and companionship, and when there are disputes and sorrow between them, it is permitted to divorce. However, the spouses must do everything in their power to keep the covenant of their marriage, and they must also know that many of those who initiate divorce admit after years that their decision was mistaken. While they were quarrelling, they were sure that after the divorce their lives would change for the better; but in practice the problems and frustrations continued to accompany their lives. Even if they married again, they found that married life involves difficulties and challenges, and had they made an effort to keep their marriage, learned to look at their spouse with a good eye and sometimes to give in, they could have merited a better and happier life. Therefore efforts are made to make domestic peace.</p>\n<p>Not only this, but the Torah established an awesome mitzva: if a case occurred in which a woman began to associate with a certain man until her husband suspected that she was unfaithful to him, and he warned her not to meet with that man alone in a hidden place, and nevertheless she secluded herself with him, and the husband suspects that she was unfaithful to him and consequently she is forbidden to him, while on the other hand the woman denies that she committed adultery – in order to make peace between them, they go up to the Temple, and they write the passage of the sota, with the Names of God written in it, on parchment, and erase the passage, in which holy Names too are written, into spring water, and the sota woman drinks this water. If she was unfaithful, she is punished from Heaven and dies; if she was not unfaithful, she lives and her health improves, and the couple may continue to live together. And although there is a severe prohibition against erasing holy Names, Hashem commanded to do so in order to make peace between them. It follows that if the holy Name is erased in order to make peace between a man and his wife, all the more so must people forgo their honour and make every effort to reconcile with each other. This includes a mitzva that the groom's parents praise their daughter-in-law, and the bride's parents praise their son-in-law, and all the more so that they not cause disputes between them.</p>\n<p>However, in a case where the man and woman did not succeed in making peace between them, they must divorce. Divorce is done by writing a get and giving it to the woman. The get is a bill of divorce written by a scribe for the divorce of so-and-so and so-and-so. Two witnesses sign the get, and the man gives it to his wife in the presence of witnesses, and from the time the woman has received the get in her hand she is divorced. In parallel to giving the get they conclude the financial affairs between them.</p>\n<p>The strongest ground for divorce is the violation of the mitzva of 'ona'. The refusal of one of the spouses to have marital relations in a pleasant and joyful way is called rebellion in marriage. Also when the husband stops supporting his family, his wife has the right to demand divorce and the full ketuba payment. However, if he is compelled by circumstances, there is room for discussion. Likewise violent or disgusting behaviour of one of the spouses is a justified ground for divorce; however, if there is a possibility of repairing the situation, the court strives to reconcile the spouses.</p>\n<p>It is fitting to note that if the spouses made an agreement from the start that neither of them is responsible for the other's support, or that the woman undertakes to support the man, their condition stands. But if they made an agreement that they are not obligated in the mitzva of ona, that is, in having marital relations in a joyful way, the marriage is void, for there is no marriage without fulfilling the mitzva of ona. Admittedly, for an elderly couple who have lived together many years, and the man has reached a state of impotence, although by the essential law the woman may demand a divorce, the court recommends to her to remain faithful to her husband.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-19/","title_he":"יט – עגונה","title_en":"19. Aguna","body_en":"<p>A married woman can be freed from her marriage and marry another man in one of two ways: either after her husband has died or after she has received a get from him. Without this she is a married woman and forbidden to every man, and if she lies with a stranger and becomes pregnant, the child born will be a mamzer (below 6:9-10). Sometimes there are sad cases in which the man is lost and it is not known whether he is alive or dead, and then his wife remains an 'aguna' (a 'chained' woman). In these cases great efforts are made to find testimony about his state, and if it can be proved that he died, his wife is released from her chained state.</p>\n<p>Sometimes the woman is right in her demand to divorce and the man is not willing to free her with a get. According to halakha the court must flog him until he gives a get willingly, that is, until he says with his mouth that he is giving the get with full will (Rambam, Hilkhot Gerushin 2:20). If not, they continue to beat him until he agrees to give a get willingly. Admittedly, there are poskim who are doubtful how far it is possible to compel a get, and because of the weakness of the courts their position raises difficulties in obtaining a get in cases of especially stubborn husbands. The problem grew when, according to civil law, it is not possible to flog the husband. Despite this, efforts are made to find ways to enforce the decision of the court on refusing men, and almost always they succeed. The more the legislators and the courts increase the cooperation between them, the fewer the hard cases of women refused a get will become.</p>\n<p>A woman too can chain her husband, for as long as she does not agree to receive the get, he cannot marry. In practice the number of husbands held back from a get is greater than the number of women refused a get; and in this matter too, for various reasons, the courts sometimes delay imposing punishments on the refusing women, and the period of being chained grows longer. But the chaining of men is lighter than the chaining of women, because in special cases, through a long process involving the consent of a hundred rabbis, he will be permitted to marry. In addition, if he begets a child without having divorced his wife, the child will not be a mamzer.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-04-20","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-04-20/","title_he":"כ – הדינים שבהם מעמד האישה חלש","title_en":"20. The Laws in Which the Woman's Standing Is Weaker","body_en":"<p>In four laws the woman's standing is weaker than the man's: a) A married woman who did not receive a get, lay with another man and bore a child – the child is a mamzer. Whereas a married man who lay with an unmarried woman and they bore a child – the child is not a mamzer. b) A married woman who was unfaithful to her husband is forbidden to her husband and to the man with whom she lay (in practice one should ask a rabbi what to do). Whereas a married man who was unfaithful with a strange woman is not forbidden to his wife. c) An aguna whose husband was lost may not marry because she is possibly married; whereas a husband whose wife was lost and they did not succeed in locating her may, by a special permission of the court, marry, since by the essential law in a time of pressing need there is no prohibition for a man to marry two wives. d) For this reason, if a woman does not agree to receive a get, in a time of pressing need, after a long process involving the permission of a hundred rabbis, her husband will be permitted to marry an additional wife without divorcing his first wife. But when the man does not agree to give his wife a get, there is no possibility of permitting his wife to marry another man without a get from her husband. Admittedly, as we learned in the previous section, there is a solution for this case: to punish the man until he agrees to give his wife a get.</p>\n<p>The laws of God are higher than our grasp, and therefore we will never be able to understand them to the end; but one cannot ignore one fundamental aspect. The woman always knows who her child is, for he was born from her womb; but the man may be in doubt whether his wife became pregnant by him or, Heaven forbid, by a stranger. And if he is in doubt about this, he will not want to work and support his wife and children, will estrange himself from them, and of course will not want to invest in their education. Through the severe laws regarding a woman who was unfaithful, and that without a get she may not marry another, the Torah assures the man that his wife is faithful to him and her children are his children, and he can devote his life to his wife and children without concern. It is hard to suppose that without this it would be possible to maintain a stable family. Moreover, a woman is by nature more faithful to her children: she carries them in her womb nine months, afterwards nurses them, and consequently also cares for them more; whereas the man's natural bond with his children exists only through love, faithfulness and moral responsibility towards them. Through these laws the man is bound to his wife and children and devotes himself to their support and well-being.</p>\n<p>The situation in secular society, which abolished these laws according to the conception of outward equality, proves the importance of these laws, without which the family unit falls apart. One can say that halakha does not deal with equality between man and woman, but shapes the most fitting way to benefit the man, the woman, the children and the whole family. Therefore in these areas it was stricter in the woman's obligations, and in the areas of livelihood, as we learned above, it was stricter in the man's obligations.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
